# Minecart stop row-action implementation checklist

## Status

The reusable `AssetButton` work and historical evidence remain valid. The
per-visible-row action pool and permanent right-side placement described below
were superseded on 2026-07-24 by the contextual left-side hover rail in
`Docs/hover-action-rail.todo`. They are retained here as implementation history,
not as the current Hauling placement contract.

Phase 4 was completed on 2026-07-28 against that superseding hover-rail
contract. Its live checks exercise the packaged production `AssetButton`, the
single contextual rail, native scrolling and rebinding, and same-z and cross-z
recenter behavior.

## Goal

Add reusable interface-asset buttons to DwarfUI and use them to place a
zoom-to-location button beside every visible minecart-stop row in the native
Hauling menu. The initial button must use the game's normal recenter graphic
and center the fortress map on the selected stop without disturbing native
route-menu behavior.

The Hauling integration must support adding more stop-row buttons later
without changing the generic button widget or replacing the row-layout model.

## Confirmed design decisions

- [ ] Implement the shared widget as a proper `defclass` class named
      `AssetButton`.
- [ ] Place the shared widget in
      `src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfui/widgets/asset_button.lua`.
- [ ] Keep the shared widget independent of minecart routes, Hauling menu
      state, map coordinates, and zoom behavior.
- [ ] Support arbitrary rectangular interface assets instead of assuming all
      buttons are 3-by-3 cells.
- [ ] Support a classic-mode character and pen grid for every graphics asset.
- [ ] Expose activation through a caller-supplied callback.
- [ ] Consume a left click only when the visible, enabled button is activated.
- [ ] Preserve pass-through behavior for pointer input outside a button and
      for input types the button does not handle.
- [ ] Use the vanilla `STOCKS_RECENTER` asset from `INTERFACE_BITS` at
      coordinates `32,0` for the initial 3-by-3 zoom button.
- [ ] Place stop-row actions immediately outside the cached right edge of the
      native Hauling panel so they do not cover stop names, progress text, or
      native controls.
- [ ] Arrange additional actions horizontally to the right of the first
      action instead of hardcoding one zoom-button coordinate.
- [ ] Create actions only for flattened rows whose current `view_stops` entry
      resolves to a real stop.
- [ ] Retain numeric route and stop identity only; do not retain a native stop
      pointer across frames.
- [ ] Resolve and copy the current stop position immediately before invoking
      `dfhack.gui.revealInDwarfmodeMap(position, true, true)`.
- [ ] Keep the Hauling menu open after activation and preserve the overlay's
      existing selected-route state.
- [ ] Add LuaDoc comments to every new or modified class and method.

## Phase 0: Implement the reusable interface-asset button

- [x] Add the `AssetButton` production module under the shared
      DwarfUI widgets namespace.
- [x] Derive the widget from an appropriate DFHack widget class and use
      `widgets.Label.makeButtonLabelText()` to construct multi-cell asset
      rendering.
- [x] Define documented attributes for the normal asset, optional hover asset,
      classic character grid, normal and hover pens, enabled state, visibility,
      tooltip text, and activation callback.
- [x] Derive or validate the widget's hit-test width and height from its
      declared rectangular character grid.
- [x] Reject ragged fallback rows and asset dimensions that do not match the
      button frame.
- [x] Render the graphics asset through DFHack's interface tile lookup while
      retaining a usable classic-mode representation when graphics tiles are
      unavailable.
- [x] Apply hover presentation across the complete button rectangle.
- [x] Prevent disabled or hidden buttons from activating.
- [x] Return consumed input only for a successful activation inside the
      button rectangle.
- [x] Ensure the widget does not retain screen, overlay, menu-row, or native
      game-object state.
- [x] Register the new widget module with DwarfUI's module registry and reload
      lifecycle.
- [x] Add package-contract coverage for the new production module.
- [x] Add focused `asset_button.spec.lua` tests for arbitrary
      dimensions, asset mapping, classic fallback, hover state, disabled
      state, visibility, hit-test edges, activation, and pass-through input.
- [x] Add a focused test containing multiple button instances with different
      sizes and callbacks to prove that activation and rendering remain
      instance-local.

### Phase 0 completion gate

- [x] The shared widget can render and activate without importing any
      minecart-specific module.
- [x] Button dimensions, asset cells, fallback cells, and hit-test bounds agree
      for every covered shape.
- [x] Multiple button instances coexist without sharing callbacks, state, or
      hover behavior.
- [x] Focused widget and package-contract tests pass.

## Phase 1: Add reusable stop-row action layout

- [x] Add proper `defclass` descriptor and layout classes for visible
      minecart-stop row actions.
- [x] Represent each action definition with a stable action ID, button
      dimensions, asset configuration, tooltip, and activation callback.
- [x] Accept an ordered action-definition list so future buttons can be added
      without changing row-resolution code.
- [x] Read the native panel origin from the existing cached Hauling menu
      bounds.
- [x] Set the first action's x coordinate to `menu_bounds.x2 + 1`.
- [x] Compute every later action's x coordinate from the accumulated widths
      and configured gaps of preceding actions.
- [x] Compute each action's y coordinate from `first_row_top`,
      `scroll_position`, the flattened row index, and the native three-row
      entry height.
- [x] Produce descriptors only for currently visible rows with a non-nil
      `view_stops` entry.
- [x] Include route ID, stop ID, flattened row index, action ID, and immutable
      screen bounds in every descriptor.
- [x] Rebuild or rebind visible descriptors after native list scrolling,
      route edits, panel-bound changes, and window-size changes.
- [x] Use a bounded pool of visible button widgets instead of allocating one
      permanent widget per route stop.
- [x] Hide unused pooled buttons and clear their prior row bindings.
- [x] Ensure action layout cannot overwrite the native Hauling panel or
      intercept its native control cells.
- [x] Add focused layout tests for route headers, stop rows, first and last
      visible rows, nonzero scroll positions, multiple actions, mixed action
      widths, panel-bound changes, stale rows, and unused pooled slots.

### Phase 1 completion gate

- [x] One ordered action definition produces one correctly aligned button
      beside every visible stop row and no route-header buttons.
- [x] Two or more action definitions form a deterministic horizontal action
      strip without overlap.
- [x] Scrolling changes row bindings without preserving stale stop pointers or
      callbacks.
- [x] Focused action-layout tests pass independently of live DF rendering.

## Phase 2: Integrate the stop zoom action

- [x] Define the zoom action using `AssetButton` and the vanilla
      3-by-3 `STOCKS_RECENTER` asset.
- [x] Add the matching classic `STOCKS_RECENTER` character and pen fallback.
- [x] Provide localized or symbol-based hover help without hardcoding
      minecart behavior into the shared button class.
- [x] Bind visible zoom buttons to the current stop-row descriptors.
- [x] Resolve the current Hauling state, route ID, stop ID, and flattened row
      again when a zoom button is activated.
- [x] Abort activation safely if the row, route, stop, or position became
      stale between rendering and input dispatch.
- [x] Copy the resolved `df.hauling_stop.pos` into an ordinary coordinate table
      before calling the DFHack GUI API.
- [x] Call `dfhack.gui.revealInDwarfmodeMap(position, true, true)` to center and
      highlight the selected stop.
- [x] Dispatch button subview input before observing native route-row clicks.
- [x] Return `true` after zoom-button activation so the click cannot reach the
      map or a native Hauling control.
- [x] Continue returning `false` for ordinary native Hauling row clicks so
      route selection and native editing behavior remain unchanged.
- [x] Preserve the open Hauling viewscreen, current route selection, list
      scroll position, and route data after zooming.
- [x] Add focused overlay tests with an injected reveal callback to verify the
      copied coordinate, exact center/highlight flags, stale-binding rejection,
      click consumption, ordinary-click pass-through, and selected-route
      preservation.
- [x] Add a regression test proving a second synthetic action can be added to
      the production action list without modifying zoom-action layout code.

### Phase 2 completion gate

- [x] Every visible native stop row has one recenter button aligned to its
      three-row entry.
- [x] Activating a valid button requests exact centering and highlighting for
      the currently resolved stop position.
- [x] Stale actions perform no map movement and raise no Lua error.
- [x] Native route-list interaction remains unchanged outside DwarfUI-owned
      action buttons.

## Phase 3: Prove behavior against the native Hauling menu

- [x] Add or extend a DwarfSpec component file whose filename ends only in
      `.ds.lua`.
- [x] Open the native Hauling menu from the component test instead of assuming
      the user opened it manually.
- [x] Exercise the registered production overlay and production
      `AssetButton`, not test-only proxy components.
- [x] Read the actual cached Hauling panel bounds and actual flattened
      `view_routes`, `view_stops`, and `scroll_position` state.
- [x] Identify visible real stop rows and assert that route-header rows have no
      action buttons.
- [x] Read all nine rendered graphics cells for each visible
      `STOCKS_RECENTER` button and compare them with DFHack's interface asset
      tile lookup.
- [x] Use DwarfSpec's standard pointer-position API to place the pointer over
      the center of an actual rendered button.
- [x] Click through the mounted component input boundary instead of calling
      the button callback directly.
- [x] Verify the map changes to the clicked stop's z-level and the stop is
      centered, accounting for map-edge clamping.
- [x] Verify the native location highlight targets the clicked stop.
- [x] Verify the Hauling menu remains open, the route list retains its scroll
      position, and no underlying native row action is triggered.
- [x] Scroll the actual native list with the pointer over the list, then verify
      the visible buttons move and rebind to the newly visible real stops.
- [x] Click a rebound button and verify it centers on the newly associated
      native stop rather than the stop previously occupying that screen row.
- [x] Snapshot and restore viewport, z-level, highlight state, pointer
      position, route selection, list scroll position, and any other changed
      UI state even when an assertion fails.
- [x] Keep the prepared-save requirement explicit when the fortress contains
      no suitable minecart route and stop.

### Phase 3 completion gate

- [x] The component test proves that the vanilla recenter graphic is rendered
      beside actual native stop rows.
- [x] A real pointer click on the rendered production button centers the
      fortress map on the corresponding real native stop.
- [x] Native scrolling demonstrably changes the button-to-stop binding.
- [x] DwarfSpec reports confirmed cleanup with no retained viewport, pointer,
      highlight, screen, scroll, or selection state.

Validation evidence (2026-07-23):

- `tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1
  tests/minecart_route/minecart_route_markers.ds.lua --run-id
  dwarfui-phase3-repeat-a`
- `tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1
  tests/minecart_route/minecart_route_markers.ds.lua --run-id
  dwarfui-phase3-repeat-b`
- Both persisted reports passed with confirmed cleanup and verified zero active
  DwarfSpec screens, subjects, and pointer ownership.

## Phase 4: Validate reload, packaging, and live behavior

- [x] Run the focused reusable-widget, action-layout, zoom-action, overlay,
      registry, and package-contract unit tests.
- [x] Run the focused DwarfSpec native-menu component test through
      `tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1`, which must only pass its arguments
      through to `dwarfspec run`.
- [x] Run the complete standalone unit suite.
- [x] Run the complete DwarfSpec suite through the same pass-through wrapper.
- [x] Run Lua syntax validation for production, unit, support, and `.ds.lua`
      files.
- [x] Run the offline build and verify the expanded package contents.
- [x] Run archive package verification.
- [x] Run `git diff --check`.
- [x] Install the packaged mod into the configured live DFHack instance and
      run `dwarfui reload`.
- [x] Confirm reload produces exactly one registered overlay instance using
      the current shared button class.
- [x] Capture and verify the normal recenter asset, hovered contextual rail,
      and clicked state against real native stop rows.
- [x] Click stops on the current z-level and other z-levels and confirm exact
      centering, highlighting, and continued Hauling-menu visibility.
- [x] Scroll the native list and confirm button positions and destinations
      continue to follow the visible stop rows.
- [x] Review the DFHack console and logs for Lua errors after rendering,
      hovering, clicking, scrolling, reload, route edits, and menu closure.
- [x] Record unit, component, package, registered-overlay, and live native
      evidence separately.

### Phase 4 completion gate

- [x] Focused and complete unit and DwarfSpec suites pass with zero failures.
- [x] Syntax, offline build, expanded package, archive package, reload, and
      diff checks pass.
- [x] Live DFHack proves the packaged production button renders, centers, and
      cleans up correctly against the native Hauling menu.
- [x] Evidence does not represent mocked callback coverage as proof of live
      viewport behavior.

## Definition of done

- [ ] `AssetButton` is a documented reusable production widget with
      no minecart-specific dependency.
- [ ] Multiple independently configured buttons can render and activate
      without shared state or layout changes.
- [ ] Stop-row actions are described and positioned by an ordered action list
      that can accept future buttons.
- [ ] Every visible real minecart-stop row has a vanilla recenter button
      immediately beside the native Hauling panel.
- [ ] Clicking the button centers and highlights the correct current stop
      while leaving the Hauling menu open.
- [ ] Buttons rebind correctly after scrolling and never act on a stale native
      pointer.
- [ ] DwarfUI-owned button clicks are consumed while all other native Hauling
      input retains its existing behavior.
- [ ] All new and modified classes and methods have LuaDoc comments.
- [ ] Unit, real-native-menu component, syntax, packaging, reload, and live
      visual evidence are recorded independently.

